+# SAM 2: separating a statue from its background
+
+[← Home](https://github.com/YukiUmetsu/kumiki-blender/wiki) · [How 3D Reconstruction Works](https://github.com/YukiUmetsu/kumiki-blender/wiki/How-3D-Reconstruction-Works)
+
+**SAM 2** stands for **Segment Anything Model 2**. It is an AI model from Meta that can identify which *pixels* in an image belong to an object you point out. The result is called a **segmentation mask**.
+
+Imagine carefully cutting a statue out of a photograph with scissors. SAM 2 tries to draw that cut-out for you. It works on ordinary photographs and can also track selected objects across video frames.
+
+**Important: SAM 2 does not create a 3D statue.** It identifies the subject in a **2D image**. Other tools use the photographs and camera positions to reconstruct 3D shape or train a Gaussian splat.
+
+## What is a mask?
+
+A **mask** is usually a black-and-white image the same size as its photograph:
+
+| Mask color | Meaning in the Kumiki Blender foreground workflow |
+| --- | --- |
+| **White (255)** | Keep these pixels: ideally the statue |
+| **Black (0)** | Ignore these pixels: ideally sky, sand, people, buildings, and other background |
+
+For the Great Sphinx, the white area should include **every visible part**: face, headdress, neck, torso, front paws, and tail. A mask containing just the face is not good enough for a full-body reconstruction.
+
+## How SAM 2 works (without the mathematics)
+
+1. **Look at the whole image.** A neural network processes the photograph into features that describe visual patterns such as edges, textures, and object boundaries.
+2. **Give it a prompt.** You can draw a **box** around the statue or click **positive points** on parts you want to keep. **Negative points** tell it what to exclude.
+3. **Predict a mask.** A mask decoder uses the image features and your prompts to propose object regions. Several possible masks can be scored.
+4. **Correct it.** If the body disappears or a pyramid is included, add more boxes and clicks, then generate a new candidate.
+5. **Review the pixels.** SAM 2 can be confident and still wrong. Check the real photograph and all the statue parts before proceeding.
+
+For example, on one Sphinx photograph:
+
+- Draw one box around the **head** and another around the **torso** and **paws**.
+- Put positive points on the actual stone of the torso, paws, and tail, where visible.
+- Put negative points on the **sky, sand, pyramid, tourists, and scaffolding**.
+- Inspect the resulting outline at full resolution; restore missing stone with corrected prompts or manually reviewed polygon edits.
+
+Kumiki Blender's SAM 2.1 integration can segment separate annotated anatomy regions and **combine** them into one mask. This is intended to avoid the common failure of selecting the head but losing the long body. Even so, automatic segmentation cannot guarantee that every visible part survives.
+
+## How it fits into 3D reconstruction
+
+The workflow is roughly:
+
+1. Collect multiple photographs showing the same statue from overlapping angles.
+2. **Register the cameras** using COLMAP so the program knows where each picture was taken.
+3. Use SAM 2.1 to create **foreground-mask candidates** for the registered, undistorted photographs.
+4. Compare masks visually and across registered views. Correct missing anatomy and background leakage.
+5. **Only after the masks pass review and quality gates**, allow a 3D reconstruction or OpenSplat training step to use them.
+
+The mask helps prevent sand, tourists, and buildings from being learned as part of the statue. But it cannot invent a missing side view, repair bad camera positions, or turn a Gaussian splat into an editable polygon mesh.
+
+### Why compare multiple views?
+
+Suppose a feature on the Sphinx's torso appears in five registered photos. If four masks keep it but one removes it, the camera-registration data can identify a **possible disagreement**. This is a useful *review suggestion*, not proof that the majority is correct: a matched point might lie on a background object or on a surface hidden in another picture. Kumiki Blender does **not** automatically repaint masks from such votes.
+
+The Sphinx mask QA checks include very small or oversized foreground regions, missing annotated anatomy, violated positive/negative points, and cross-view feature disagreements. For SAM 2.1, you also explicitly mark whether each of the six statue parts is **visible** or **not visible** in each photo. Uncertain or inconsistent annotations block training.
+
+## Trying it on a Mac (Apple Silicon)
+
+For an M4 Mac with 24 GB memory, start with **SAM 2.1 small**. Try **tiny** if you need a lighter model, or CPU mode if an operation fails on Metal/MPS. You need the optional PyTorch/SAM 2 installation and an official **local checkpoint**; Kumiki Blender does not silently download SAM 2 weights.
+
+From the `kumiki-blender` project folder:
+
+```bash
+python3 -m pip install -e '.[mask-sam2]'
+git clone https://github.com/facebookresearch/sam2.git ../sam2
+SAM2_BUILD_CUDA=0 python3 -m pip install -e ../sam2
+```
+
+Then download the correct `sam2.1_hiera_small.pt` checkpoint from the [official SAM 2 repository](https://github.com/facebookresearch/sam2#download-checkpoints). Create a per-photo JSON guide from [the Sphinx example](https://github.com/YukiUmetsu/kumiki-blender/blob/main/examples/sphinx-sam2-guides.example.json). The example coordinates are **not** accurate annotations for your own photographs.
+
+```bash
+# Replace the placeholders with your real immutable splat revision,
+# reviewed guide file, and downloaded checkpoint paths.
+SOURCE=.review/mac-splat-smoke/splat-REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_REVISION
+
+MASK_GUIDES=/absolute/path/to/sphinx-guides.json \
+SAM2_CHECKPOINT=/absolute/path/to/sam2.1_hiera_small.pt \
+SAM2_SIZE=small SAM2_DEVICE=auto PYTORCH_ENABLE_MPS_FALLBACK=1 \
+  bash scripts/mac_splat_test.sh foreground-prep "$SOURCE" sam2
+```
+
+This prepares **new candidate mask, cross-view audit, and quality-report revisions**. It does **not** start OpenSplat training.
+
+Open the generated `mask-contact-sheet.jpg` and `mask-consistency-contact-sheet.jpg`. A bad mask should be corrected in a **new revision**, preserving the original photographs, camera registration, and previous masks.
+
+For exact guide fields, correction tools, quality thresholds, and the complete macOS commands, see the [technical foreground-masking workflow](https://github.com/YukiUmetsu/kumiki-blender/blob/main/docs/GAUSSIAN_SPLATS.md#foreground-first-pipeline-recommended-for-large-statues).
+
+## What SAM 2 cannot solve
+
+- **It does not know the Sphinx perfectly.** Prompting the head alone can remove the torso, paws, or tail.
+- **It may confuse similar colors and textures.** Weathered stone and sand can look alike.
+- **It cannot recover invisible geometry.** Missing views remain missing.
+- **Its video memory is not 3D consistency.** SAM 2's ability to track masks through video does not automatically make unrelated photographs agree in 3D.
+- **It is not a reconstruction quality certificate.** Pixel masks, camera alignment, mesh geometry, and final renders all need their own checks.
+
+## Learn more (original sources)
+
+- [Meta: Segment Anything Model 2](https://ai.meta.com/research/sam2/) — overview and illustrations.
+- [Official SAM 2 code and checkpoints](https://github.com/facebookresearch/sam2) — implementation, installation, and examples.
+- [SAM 2 research paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.00714) — technical details of the model.
+
+**Next:** [How 3D Reconstruction Works](https://github.com/YukiUmetsu/kumiki-blender/wiki/How-3D-Reconstruction-Works) explains how camera matching, point clouds, meshes, and Gaussian splatting use the photographs after segmentation.
